==English==
{{was wotd|2007|June|19}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*swep-}}
First attested in 1615. Borrowed from {{bor|en|fr|somnolent}}, from {{der|en|fro|sompnolent}},  from {{der|en|la|somnolentus}}, from {{m|la|somnus||sleep}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*swépnos}}, {{m|ine-pro|*supnós||dream}}, which both are derived from {{m|ine-pro|*swep-}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈsɒmnələnt/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈsɑːmnələnt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-somnolent.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[drowsy|Drowsy]] or [[sleepy]].
#* {{RQ:Pynchon Crying Lot|page=60|chapter=4|passage=One minute she was gazing at a mockup of a space capsule, safely surrounded by old, '''somnolent''' men; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author={{w|Michiko Kakutani}}|title=Just 30 Years Later, Updike Has a Quartet|work=The New York Times|date=1990-09-25|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1990/09/25/books/books-of-the-times-just-30-years-later-updike-has-a-quartet.html|issn=0362-4331|passage={{...|Taken together, this quartet of novels has given its readers a wonderfully vivid portrait of one Harry (Rabbit) Angstrom, a small-town Pennsylvania basketball star turned car salesman}}; and in chronicling the passing parade of Harry's life, the books have also created a Kodachrome-sharp picture of American life - the psychic ups and downs, enthusiasms and reversals experienced by this nation as it moved from the '''somnolent''' 50's through the upheavals of the 60's and 70's into the uncertainties of the 80's.}}
#*{{quote-book|en|date=Apr 1 1992|author=Bill Watterson|title=Calvin & Hobbes|genre=comic|passage=My tiger is deep<br />in '''somnolent''' sleep<br />dreaming of chases remembered!<br />His keen eyes are glinting!<br />He dreams of a sprinting<br />sambar who'll soon be dismembered!}}
#*{{quote-book|en|year=1999|author=Stephen King|title=The Girl Who Loved Tom Gordon|passage=As she reached toward the last few fiddleheads growing on the second hummock, her hand froze. She heard the '''somnolent''' buzzing of flies again.}}
# {{lb|en|dated}} Causing literal or figurative [[sleepiness]].
#: {{syn|en|soporific|Thesaurus:soporific}}

